Hi Simon,

Thanks so much for updating the note. It's looking really good. Just two things.

At [40][footnote 13][49a.], PG previously applied a 60%&nbsp;factor to incurred costs (Rowe &amp; Ors v Ingenious Media Holdings plc &amp; Ors&nbsp;[2020] EWHC 235 (Ch) and
B v CD&nbsp;[2021] EWHC 741 (Comm)). This time, we have applied 50%, given the criticisms the Defendants themselves made against each other on incurred costs (notably Mr Andrew Lyons against Mercedes).

I’m still struggling to follow the logic of why the Claimants would need to secure LOI for the Defendants’ work incurred post–July 2026&nbsp;(i.e. after the PDD judgment).I’m not suggesting a further SFC application in July, my thinking is that one would never be
 required.
&nbsp;
My understanding is as follows:
A. Claimants Win PDD (wholly or partially)


No (or limited) adverse costs order for PDD in Defendants'&nbsp;favour → some or all existing ATE cover remains unused&nbsp;for that purpose.



Quantum trial proceeds in October, but the PDD-era ATE resource can be “recycled”&nbsp;to cover the Defendants future adverse costs exposure (e.g. Quantum PTR, Trial Prep, Trial).



Net result:&nbsp;No need for additional&nbsp;ATE or LOI cover for post July 2026 if the existing cover (from PDD) exceeds or matches quantum-phase risk (which does).

B. Claimants Lose PDD


Adverse costs order for PDD&nbsp;→ Current ATE is needed to satisfy that liability;&nbsp;therefore Claimants need sufficient ATE to cover costs up to July 2026.



Quantum trial will not happen, since it follows logically from PDD success.



Therefore: Quantum PTR, Trial Prep, and Trial phases will never be incurred -&nbsp;and there is no risk of further adverse costs&nbsp;after July 2026.



Net result:&nbsp;No additional ATE or LOI needed beyond July 2026 (and any already-obtained for post-July 2026&nbsp;may be wasted, i.e. irrecoverable premium expense).


Either way, the conclusion seems to be that no further ATE or LOI cover is required for the Defendants’ adverse costs post–July 2026 on any outcome.


Happy to jump on a call and be corrected if I’m missing the point somewhere.


