itlements?Scope - how exactly will cross-collateralisation operate i.e., what types of insurer recoveries (e.g., paid claims, contingent premiums) are in scope?Existing arrangements - would there be any impact on the wrapper arrangements already in place? Can we keep them completely separate?We would also like to explore the Mosaic option you mentioned yesterday in tandem. Please could you provide some additional detail on the following points: 1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Structure - the policy would need to safeguard client interests, could the policy expressly designate claimants as third-party beneficiaries? If not, would it be possible for the insurer to issue a side letter, deed of benefit, or assignment giving clients direct enforceable rights? &nbsp;2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claims - it would be helpful if you could outline the claims process, including how coverage applies when the insured is not the named party to the adverse costs order.3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Protections for PG – we would need to ensure that the firm is not liable if the insurer declines cover for reasons unrelated to misconduct. How can PG navigate this risk?4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Claim payments - this structure could be scrutinised by the defendants and therefore it would assist if any claims monies could be directly paid to the defendant or PGs client account.&nbsp;Many thanks Helena &nbsp;​​​​Helena&nbsp;YearwoodRisk&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Compliance&nbsp;DirectorThis email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited l