iform risk level. To address this – they have suggested a form of cross-collateralisation agreement that would sit over the eight top-up policies. Would this in principle be acceptable to PG? I note there are already the BHP reimbursement deed and the wrapper agreement that relate to the proceeds on these matters, which will need to be taken into account here. Having a cross-collateralisation agreement in place would push the pricing offered downwards.Pricing – it is likely to be relatively expensive given they are insuring these matters during a trial and so the upfront premium is likely to begin with a “2”. They would be willing to split this into say two payments (the latter of which would be payable in any event) but splitting the payments would increase the overall price e.g. 24% ROL upfront or 2x 14% ROL staggered payments (NB these numbers are just illustrative rather than the pricing that Toremis will provide). By way of comparison, where pricing can be offered during trial we have seen it be offered at 30+%.If point 1 and 2 are acceptable, would we be able to set up the requested call with Toremis this week?&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global &nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116) On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.com www.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &amp; Acquisitions, Professional Indemnity, and Operational Risks within WTW. Part of our wider risk solutions capabilities at WTW&nbsp;At