Regarding providing Tom Goodhead’s personal data (his email address), these are what we could say to the court.

First, we have considered the case of&nbsp;Phones4U Ltd v EE Ltd &amp; ors&nbsp;[2021] EWCA Civ 116. In that case, it appeared that there was disclosable material on the personal devices
 of employees and ex-employees of some defendants, which were cooperate entities. The judge at first instance ordered the defendants to&nbsp;askthe employees and ex-employees to turn over their personal devices to the defendants’ IT consultant, which would
 search for work-related materials [para 2]. The order was upheld on appeal.

In that case, the Court did not order the defendants to provide any details of the employees and ex-employees to the claimant, even though the defendants were in fact under
 a duty of disclosure – which PG is not in this case – and it could be argued that, in allowing the employees and ex-employees to use personal devices to store work-related materials on their personal phones, the defendants had either purposefully relinquished
 control over documents (including in respect of&nbsp;current&nbsp;employees) or assumed the risk that their disclosure duties would run to their employees’ personal devices.

I note also that the Court acknowledged that if the employees and ex-employees did not accede to the request, then the claimant would need to adopt some other approach, which
 could include an order for third party disclosure [para 51].

In this case, the legal obligation is on Tom Goodhead, not the firm (save to do what is reasonable). An obligation could easily have been put on the firms in the CRO documents,
 but the parties chose not to do so. Therefore, by the logic of&nbsp;Phones4U, the most a party can be required to do is ask its ex-employees for assistance, which is what PG has done repeatedly.

As to whether it is in fact reasonable for PG to provided Goodhead’s details, we say that is highly likely to be a breach of data protection rules, and it is not reasonable
 to act unlawfully.

As for the data protection: providing Goodhead’s email address would be processing his personal data, and PG would need a lawful basis for doing so under article 6 of the GDPR.
 Obviously it cannot use consent, since Goodhead has never provided it – and his silence would be considered as refusal in the scheme of data protection. It is also significant that PG could not rely on the compliance with legal obligation basis (art 6(1)(c)),
 as the CROs cannot in any way be read to require PG to hand over such personal data.

This is significant, because there is only one potential basis PG could use to hand over Goodhead’s data: that it is&nbsp;necessary&nbsp;to do so for PG or the Ds’ legitimate interests,&nbsp;and&nbsp;that
 those interests are not overridden by Goodhead’s interests or fundamental rights and freedoms (art 6(1)(f)). As we stand, we do not think we could satisfy this test for the following reasons:


It is not necessary because

Ds have other ways of contacting Mr Goodhead – via LinkedIn (Thomas Goodhead | LinkedIn)
 or through physical addresses listed at Companies House (thomas
 goodhead - Find and update company information - GOV.UK)
An email from them to him does not guarantee anything. He can just as easily ignore their emails as he has ours.

Our and Ds’ legitimate interests exist but may not be so strong:

The court declined to order us to disclose the data, even though we said we would have no objection to an application requiring that.
There is no legal obligation on us to disclose it arising from the CROs or otherwise

Goodhead has an interest in not having this data disclosed, as is clearly his belief, since he has refused to let us provide it.
We understand Goodhead to be litigious, and there is a real risk of him taking action against us for unauthorised disclosure of his personal data.

&nbsp;

Ultimately, this can all be resolved, without putting us in jeopardy of legal action, if the court simply orders us to release the data.
