- 9 October: Michael Burke sent an email to the board, on behalf of himself, me and others, explaining that we were stressed and traumatised.
- 2 November: I raised a formal grievance with the firm in relation to my stress, explaining that I had not received even a bare acknowledgement of my stress, let alone an apology. The grievance stated that I was particularly worried about how I would be treated
 from 28 November onwards, when the PCD section of the trial finished, and from which point I would be &quot;dead weight&quot;.


- 4 November: I worked until 3am (such late hours were not unusual in trial preparation), as Anna Varga was aware from contemporaneous emails and Teams messages.
- Friday 14 November: the working day before my section of the trial began, the firm replied to my grievance, stating that the grievance procedure was an inappropriate mechanism&nbsp;for the issues that I raised.
- Friday 14 November, 16:27: Sarah Bingham, who has resigned, submitted a note from her doctor resulting in her being signed off from work.
- Monday 17 November: the first day of the PCD trial.&nbsp; Anna Varga sent an email to all emissions staff commenting on the amount of work done by the PCD team over the weekend.&nbsp; I was exhausted, as was the entire solicitor and counsel team, and kept going.


- Monday 17 November, 09:59: the firm uploaded to &quot;HiBob&quot; a &quot;Sickness Absence Policy&quot;, which states that employees are only entitled to &quot;Occupational Sick Pay&quot; (OSP) as opposed to Statutory Sick Pay if &quot;the employee has not given notice
 to terminate their employment.&quot;


- As above, the firm has never before had a Sickness Absence Policy, or any policy stating that OSP is only available to employees who have not resigned.
- I was not told about the policy's introduction. No firm email or communication was sent about the policy's upload, introduction or terms.


- The firm's position is that that policy upload amounted to a unilateral change to the terms of my employment contract, without notice or consent.


- I was not told that the firm believed that the terms of my employment contract had been unilaterally changed.


- 17-27 November: I led the PCD trial for PG, attending Court every day and with conduct of the case for PG.&nbsp; Ben Croft (Leigh Day partner leading PCD for LD) was unwell and only attended Court for two days, so in practice I was the most
 senior Lead Solicitor in Court with carriage of the case.&nbsp; The responsibility was serious, and I took it seriously.&nbsp; At the same time I worked on a handover of the PCD case to Oliver Shipway.


- As per a Teams message with Anna Varga during this period, I believe that a few intense days of time recording might be missing, and evening work during trial.&nbsp; I have nevertheless recorded over 2,000 hours for this year, with a month
 to go, and similar hours for last year.&nbsp; As per my resignation letter, the hours recorded do not reflect, for example, regular mental health check-ins with members of my team.&nbsp; Anna Varga was copied to some relevant emails so has some awareness of the hours
 that I worked during this period.


- 28 November: I sent the firm a doctor's note that I am not fit to work, because of stress, burnout, and exhaustion, until 15 December.
- 1 December: the firm told me that I would not be paid OSP for my sick leave, and instead only SSP. The firm locked me out of PG systems, preventing me from self-certifying as fit to work, working, and thus entitling myself to pay. The firm has previously
 refused me leaving for JLG early.
- The consequence is that the firm is attempting to impose on me more than a fortnight of no pay. The firm has now confirmed that it does not want me to provide a handover, having previously repeatedly chased me for one.


&nbsp;


Next steps

As above, please answer the questions to my below email.

I reserve the right to share this email chain with the SRA.

My understanding from your emails is that the firm's position is that I am not being treated exceptionally, but in line with how the firm would treat any employee in my position. I therefore reserve the right to share this email with third parties.

I intend to share this email and the below chain with Acas.&nbsp; This chain, my resignation letter, and my formal grievance will form the basis of my witness statement in an employment appeal tribunal (EAT) claim.

I expect that this line of correspondence is being managed, for PG, by an external firm. If I am right (which I will assume until I am told otherwise), the fees incurred by that external firm are likely more than simply paying me OSP for my sick leave and compensation
 for having had to argue for it. It is a waste of the firm's time and money to go to the EAT to attempt to defend its position, at which my witness statement will become public, rather than pay me. It is not a waste of mine.

As per the below, I will be in the office on 15 December. Please let me know if you want to discuss any outstanding issues in person then.&nbsp; In spite of the firm's treatment of staff, as explained in my resignation letter and grievance, I am open to a negotiated
 solution. However, in light of that same treatment, I am not overly hopeful that this will resolve amicably and plan to contact Acas next week.


