Hi Helena,
&nbsp;
Apologies for missing your calls, I was in a meeting this afternoon.
&nbsp;
For the moment insurers have reserved their rights in respect of any claim on the policy. They have also asked whether PG previously informed insurers about these applications and/or sought consent to contest them?
&nbsp;
Would you be able to give more information about the timing and making of the applications, the decision to contest them and their notice to insurers? I can see that there have been references to the expert reports in the periodic
 NOx updates but no express mention of the Defendants’ applications.
&nbsp;
The i.) Mercedes, and ii.) NOx wrapper policies have slightly different exclusion clauses in relation to adverse costs attributable to interim applications.
&nbsp;
The Mercedes policy excludes any claim under the policy attributable to “any interim application made or resisted without
Insurer’s Approval” (clause 2.1.4).
&nbsp;
The NOx wrapper policies (Ford, RN and CP)
excludes, unless Insurers Approval has been given, any claim under the policy attributable to:
“The Insured’s intention to apply for summary judgment under
CPR Part 24 or to make any other application which will be listed for or take half a day or more” (clause 4.1.4)
&nbsp;
The insurers have not yet sought to rely on these clauses but I would imagine these clauses are the context in which they seek further information. Given clause 4.1.4 above, it may also be that a notification was not required
 under the policy in any event.
&nbsp;
Regarding your instructions to Counsel – can you let us know the offer to settle the matters that you would like us to put to insurers for their agreement?
