conduct since submitting my notice demonstrates that I am committed to working with the firm to progress this in a professional and orderly fashion. This email has some detail in it so if it is easier for us to hop on a call to try and cut through bits of it or to have a quicker conversation, I'm more than happy to do so.&nbsp;This email is in two parts: i) to address my notice period and when may be a good time for me to depart the firm; and ii) to address on-going travel and litigation needs with a view to finding a pragmatic way to handle future travel etc.Notice PeriodI agree that we need to work collaboratively to find an appropriate point for me to leave the firm, both to ensure the clients are protected and not disadvantaged, and to ensure that I am protected and not prejudiced. My current leave date is I understand the 15 December 2025. There are various important deadlines between now and then an to add a little more colour to those deadlines, the deadlines we have approaching (demarcated as PDD or Quantum) are as follows:06.10.2025 – PTR 2 (PDD)10.10.2025 – receipt of disclosure from Defendants (Quantum)13.10.2025 – Trial starts (PDD)21.11.2025 – Individual Particulars of Quantum to be filed and served (Quantum)11.12.2025 – receipt of disclosure from Defendants (Quantum)19.12.2025 – Trial ends (PDD)19.12.2025 – Applications for January 2026 CMC (Quantum)As is evident, the litigation has a large number of critical deadlines looming. Overlayed with this, is the added complication of each OEM having its own CRO, as well as an overall Pan-NOx CRO.Each individual must apply to the Defendants (individually per CRO and to all Defendants for the Pan-Lead CRO) for permission to be entered into that CRO. This takes up to 5 working days. There is also a process for applying to be removed from each CRO which can, in itself, take some days.Given the level of confidential information in the NOx litigations, someone who is not in one, or all, of the CROs, is necessarily extremely limited as to what they can see, do and work on. For example, they could not actively participate in the trial, nor could they provide any type of meaningful input into any of the directions that fall across the period that they are not in the CRO i.e. the IPQs. If therefore I am held to work my notice until 15 December, I&nbsp;will be unable to attend trial for the final week before Christmas, nor will I will be able to really assist with any work on quantum disclosure, or work on the applications / witness statements for the January 2026 CMC.Considering this, we (both me and Pogust Goodhead) need&nbsp;to realistically consider when my departure should fall, as it should come at a time that will be at the least detriment of the claimants.To that end, it seems sensible, and would be in everyone’s interests, for me to depart Pogust Goodhead on a date that will allow me to start my new role and be re-entered into the various CROs, which does not conflict with any of those deadlines, nor run over a critical part of the trial that I am intended to participate in. To require me to serve my notice such that I leave the firm on a date which conflicts with a major deadline would be of significant detriment to the clients, not only of this firm, but to other firms as well.Having considered the timetable and what is genuinely in the best interests of everyone, especially the clients, it would appear the best date for me to depart Pogust Goodhead is the 17 October. This date falls after the opening submissions, and across the Normal Driving Conditions expert evidence (which is plainly of importance, but falls at the lower end of the scale of importance considering the other critical tasks over the trial period). Exiting Pogust Goodhead then, will allow me sufficient time to commence my new role before substantive evidence relating to the manufacturers starts.Any other date past this, will cause significant issues with my involvement over key aspects of the trial and, I believe, will have a negative impact on the claimants. For a Partner with current conduct of an ALGLO, who has detailed knowledge of all of the cases across the Pan-NOx Litigation, to be unable to participate in the trial, is not in anyone’s interests. The obvious link is that any prohibition on me being able to work on these cases, also directly impacts the Pogust Goodhead claimants and the prospects of their claims being successfully concluded.Without wishing this to become a confrontational or protracted process (as I am committed to progressing this in a professional and orderly fashion), if Pogust Goodhead wish me to remain in post past this date, I would ask that they provide good reason why, including an explanation of why they do not believe this will impact the clients (including why they believe it is in the clients best interests) including the trial timetable and the quantum deadlines.I would ask this be provided to assist me with any future queries that may be raised of me in respect of compliance with my professional and regulatory obligations i.e. &quot;why did you leave during the PDD Trial or&nbsp; why didn't you leave at a point when you would be restricted from working on critical quantum workstreams?&quot;.Future TravelWhile we resolve the issue of my notice period, I am conscious that it is “business as usual” and I am committed to this. As such, I will be needing to continue to travel to London for work in the office and for attendance at trial (while I am still employed by the firm).I appreciate the arrangement I have with the firm is that they will book my flights for me. Given I wish to make bookings into the future, past the date which I have given above as my preferred leave date, I would suggest that it is more pragmatic, to ensure they the firm is not put to more expense than is needed, for me to take charge of booking my flights, which I can then claim back through expenses. Going forward then, flights which occur over the&nbsp;dates of my employment, I would seek to recover in expenses. For any flights booked that occur past my leave date, I would then cover the cost of myself (as I would no longer be in the employ of the firm).This strikes me as inherently more sensible than continuing&nbsp;to have the firm book my flights, as in an instance where the firm has booked a flight for me which is due to take off after my leave date, I would not be able to travel on that flight, and I would need to re-book the flight most likely at a higher price.Additional to this (and at a higher cost), is the flat that the firm has rented for me the duration of the trial. This rental starts on 6 October and runs until 21 December. I am intending on using this for as long as I am with the firm. The firm however, especially if they are agreeable to my suggested leave date, may wish to consider cancelling this booking and instead booking me into hotels until my leave date. This may be something that the firm considered a more pragmatic and financially sound option. I am comfortable either way and am happy to have an open dialogue on how best to manage this.Until hearing otherwise, I will change my flight booking system such that I will make flight bookings personally and the recover the cost via expenses, and I will proceed as if to stay in that flat over the remainder of my employment.I look forward to hearing from you, and should you require anything further, please let me know.Best,Michael&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344From:&nbsp;Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;18 September 2025 1:52 PMTo:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;FW: Notice of Resignation&nbsp;Dear Michael,&nbsp;I write further to my previous email, to which I have not received a reply. For the avoidance of doubt, your contractual obligations remain in full force and effect, including your duty to serve your full notice period.&nbsp;If you have any questions, please let me know.&nbsp;RegardsRaminder&nbsp;​​​​Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718From:&nbsp;Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;15 September 2025 17:16To:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Notice of Resignation&nbsp;Dear Michael,&nbsp;I write further to your resignation email received today.&nbsp;It’s disappointing to note that you have chosen to resign, given that the Board have listened to concerns which have been raised and has engaged in a process to allay them.&nbsp; We also note the concerns you raise related to the previous leadership of the firm. The new Board is determined to bring the highest standards of propriety and integrity to the organisation and is confident that the process being undertaken will arrive at a place that will be in the best interests of the Firm, its employees and, most importantly, its clients. Given the above process, your resignation seems premature. As a result, it is with regret, we accept it.&nbsp;The Firm notes your request for an early release and your acceptance of your obligations at clause 16 of your resignation letter. Given the deadlines involved in the case you referred to, at this stage, the Firm would like you to continue working your notice to ensure that the clients are not put at a disadvantage as a result of your decision. To the same end, and at an appropriate point, we would like you to undertake an orderly handover of your files. At that point, the Firm would be happy to discuss an appropriate way forward with you. Please continue working normally in the meantime.&nbsp;Please note that the entirety of your contract of employment remains in place during your notice period. This includes the express obligations set out under clauses 4.2, 16, 17 and 21&nbsp;of the employment contract executed by you with a commencement date of 1st September 2022. Your contract also contains implicit contractual obligations as to “good faith and fidelity”.&nbsp; This includes an obligation to act honestly in all dealings with your employer, not working (or conspiring) with a competitor, not disclosing our confidential information or trade secrets, not actively disrupting the business or enticing colleagues or clients to leave.&nbsp; To this end, it is notable that a number of your colleagues have chosen to resign contemporaneously.&nbsp; Please be aware that the company will not hesitate to take robust action to enforce any breach of your obligations up to and including court action if that becomes necessary. I also bring your attention more generally to the fact that the restrictive covenants at clause 21 of your employment contract, which were varied by your agreement on 14th September 2023, continue to remain in effect for up to 6 months after your effective date of termination.&nbsp;If you should have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact me.&nbsp;Regards,Raminder&nbsp;​​​​Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Monday, September 15, 2025 10:24To:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;HR Team &lt;hr@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Notice of Resignation&nbsp;Dear Board and HR,&nbsp;Please see attached my notice of resignation.&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;Michael​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;_________________