To: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;
Cc: Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;
Subject: Notice of Resignation









Some people who received this message don't often get email from
sarahlbingham@hotmail.co.uk.
Learn why this is important










CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.

&nbsp;


Dear Raminder,

&nbsp;

I hereby resign from my position at Pogust Goodhead with immediate effect.

&nbsp;

I refer to the matters raised with the Board in various emails, letters, and meetings over the past six weeks. I appreciate this is an extremely difficult time for everyone employed and associated
 with Pogust Goodhead, and while I do not wish to add to that difficulty, I no longer feel able to continue in employment and need to step away.

&nbsp;

Although recent events have been especially challenging, the reality is that I have been unhappy at Pogust Goodhead for quite some time. This decision is not one I have taken lightly but one
 I have been contemplating for a long while. I remain grateful for the opportunities I have had at the firm and for the successes we achieved together.

&nbsp;

In order to protect both my position and that of the firm, I request to be placed on garden leave for the entirety of my notice period (Monday 15 September 2025 to Monday 15 December 2025 inclusive).
 I accept that any accrued holiday entitlement will be treated as running concurrently with garden leave.

&nbsp;

My request is based on the following considerations:

&nbsp;


BHP costs budgeting: Remaining in post would place me in the position of preparing and/or approving costs budgets
 due later this year (or be working notice when someone else does the same). The Precedent H declaration requires certification that incurred and estimated costs are a fair and accurate statement of those costs entitled and intended to be recovered. Where certain
 clients’ funding arrangements are unenforceable (unequivocally the business clients’ DBA and arguably other client cohorts), such costs cannot lawfully be recovered. To certify otherwise (or for me to be working when someone else does) would be materially
 misleading and risk professional misconduct in breach of the SRA Principles and my overriding duty to the Court.
Funding information disclosure: I am likely to be called upon to deal with disclosure requests concerning highly
 sensitive funding information as well as security for costs in both NOx and BHP. Given the confidential nature of this material, I assume such matters will involve input from the Board. Nonetheless, retaining responsibility would expose me to significant regulatory
 risk especially when I have not had access to all funding documents.&nbsp;
Standard practice: As a Legal Director with extensive access to confidential strategic and settlement information
 across all cases, it is standard practice for senior lawyers in such circumstances to be placed on garden leave. This protects client settlement, safeguards sensitive information, and ensures continuity for the firm.

&nbsp;

For these reasons, garden leave is the most appropriate arrangement. It protects my professional obligations, reduces regulatory risk for both myself and the firm, and ensures that these sensitive
 matters are handled transparently and without conflict.

Please confirm the arrangements for my notice period and the return of firm property.&nbsp;

I wish the firm and my colleagues the best for the future.

&nbsp;

Yours sincerely,

Sarah Bingham

&nbsp;



















